From: Timofey Maykov <itnitro@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] debug: stock bring-up order for UNIPHY0 and the QCA8084

A stock boot log of a BE7000 (stock 1.1.x, kernel 5.4, qca-ssdk.ko) shows
this order: mdio-qca pulses the QCA8084 GPIO reset and does its preinit at
2.55-2.77 s, so the chip is out of reset and powered from then on. The
SSDK loads at 14.3 s, resets the CMN block with the GCC clock init and the
PPE at 14.87 s, sets UNIPHY0 to UQXGMII (PLL reset, sys/soft reset, one
calibration) at 15.0-15.19 s and, in the same call, sets the chip to
PORT_UQXGMII at 15.198 s. UNIPHY2 follows at 15.29-15.88 s. The SoC XPCS
is never reset or reconfigured again after that.

The chip system clocks follow their own timeline on stock. The mdio-qca
preinit (qca_mht_clock_init, about 2.77 s) enables SRDS0_SYS_CBCR
(0xc8001a8) and SRDS1_SYS_CBCR (0xc8001ac) and pulses their resets, and
enables the four GEPHY system clocks (0xc8001b0-0xc8001bc). All six are
still on when UNIPHY0 calibrates and when the chip is set to UQXGMII.
Only after the chip mode set, at 15.262 s ("MHT GCC CLK initialization"),
does ssdk_mht_gcc_clock_init() -> ssdk_mht_gcc_common_clk_parent_enable()
run in MHT_PHY_UQXGMII_MODE: it sets SRDS0_SYS to XO/25 MHz and turns
SerDes0 off to save power, and enables SRDS1_SYS and GEPHY0-3_SYS (plus
the AHB and sec control clocks, with the switch core clock off). The open
SSDK source asserts the SerDes0 ARES there, but the stock qca-ssdk.ko
1.1.38 calls ssdk_mht_clk_disable() at that point (offset 0x13c of the
function), which clears only the enable bit. The stock runtime dump
agrees: SRDS0_SYS CBC 0x80000000, SRDS1 and GEPHY0-3 0x1.

uniphy24 calibrates UNIPHY0 at 1.51 s with the chip still in reset, only
releases the chip reset at 2.77 s (0918), and sets the chip SerDes up at
12.5 s, 11 s after the SoC calibration, then does a SoC XPCS redo on the
first link up (0906). 0918 misread SSDK commit 3c58ee12: that commit puts
the chip UQXGMII mode set after the SoC UNIPHY, not the chip GPIO reset.

With pcs_qcom_ipq9574.stock_order=1 (default):
- nsscc-qca8k ignores qcom,reset-after-pcs and does the GPIO reset, the
  settle and the vendor preinit at its own probe, as mdio-qca does. The
  PCS parameter is set before any initcall, so reading it there has no
  probe order issue.
- nsscc-qca8k holds both chip SerDes system clocks on after the preinit
  (with qcom,vendor-preinit). SerDes0 has no consumer here, and the only
  consumer of SerDes1, the QCA8084 PCS, enables it at the chip mode set.
  Without the hold clk_disable_unused() gates both at about 3.5 s, so
  UNIPHY0 calibrates with both off and the chip with SerDes0 off, where
  stock has both on. The GEPHY system clocks are not held: each QCA8084
  PHY enables its own at probe, and the deferred probes are flushed at
  late_initcall, before clk_disable_unused() at late_initcall_sync, so
  they stay on as on stock. dmesg says what is held.
- The PCS probe does not do the qcom,early-10g-qxgmii and
  qcom,idle-usxgmii bring-ups and keeps them pending.
- The QCA8084 driver logs the SRDS0, SRDS1 and GEPHY0-3 SYS_CBCRs (stock
  has all six at 0x1 there) and calls ipq_pcs_stock_soc_up() right before
  qca8084_qxgmii_set_mode(): the same early bring-up code as before (0924
  vendor order, 0913/0917), once per boot, so phylink's pcs_config later
  finds the interface set and does the per-channel part only. dmesg logs
  the time from the SoC calibration to the chip mode set.
- Right after the chip mode set the QCA8084 driver calls
  nss_cc_qca8k_stock_release(), where stock runs its MHT GCC clock init:
  the SerDes0 hold is dropped, which clears only the enable bit as the
  stock SSDK does, and dmesg shows SRDS0/SRDS1_SYS_CBCR before and after
  (stock 0x80000000 and 0x1). SerDes1 stays held. Then it reads the chip
  BaseR status for the 0903 decision and calls ipq_pcs_stock_idle_up()
  for UNIPHY2, so the 0.4 s UNIPHY2 bring-up does not change what 0903
  sees. If the package init ends before the chip mode set (an error, or no
  10G-QXGMII), UNIPHY2 is brought up anyway, with a warning, so it is not
  left parked.
- The SoC XPCS redo on first link up (0906) is skipped when the XPCS was
  last set up by that stock bring-up. If the stock bring-up did not run
  and phylink set the XPCS up, the redo runs as before. The chip-side
  redo (0903) stays.
- The UNIPHY0 monitor and edge detector (0926) start only after that
  bring-up has finished, so they never read UNIPHY0/XPCS during its PLL
  and XPCS resets.
- ipq_pcs0_ctl, ipq_pcs0_regs and ipq_pcs_idle_ctl return -EBUSY while
  the UNIPHY0 or UNIPHY2 stock bring-up runs, and neither bring-up starts
  while one of them runs.

The CMN reset at probe, the 100 ms settle, suppress_los, the other debugfs
knobs and the 0920 details stay as they are. stock_order=0 gives uniphy24.
A board that loses RX without the SoC redo can still get it by hand with
"echo xpcs > /sys/kernel/debug/ipq_pcs0_ctl".

The hooks are declared in include/linux/pcs/pcs-qcom-ipq9574.h under
IS_REACHABLE(). A built-in caller of a modular PCS, or a kernel without the
PCS, gets stubs that keep the old order. A modular PCS reports stock_order
off and keeps the old order itself, because the built-in QCA8084 drivers
cannot call into it. The 0921 ipq_pcs_debug_recalibrate() declaration moves
to that header with a stub for the same reason, so qca808x no longer needs
the PCS built in to link. nss_cc_qca8k_stock_release() sits in the same
header under IS_REACHABLE(CONFIG_IPQ_NSSCC_QCA8K); a modular nsscc-qca8k
with a built-in QCA8084 PHY driver does not take the SerDes0 hold, since
nothing could drop it.

--- a/drivers/clk/qcom/nsscc-qca8k.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/qcom/nsscc-qca8k.c
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/of_platform.h>
+#include <linux/pcs/pcs-qcom-ipq9574.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/property.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
@@ -2247,6 +2248,135 @@
 	dev_info(dev, "vendor preinit done, SRDS1_SYS_CBCR 0x%08x\n", val);
 }
 
+/* Stock order: stock mdio-qca (qca_mht_clock_init) turns both SerDes system
+ * clocks and the four GEPHY system clocks on outside the clock framework at
+ * its probe (2.77 s). They stay on through the SoC UNIPHY0 calibration and
+ * the chip UQXGMII mode set (15.2 s). Right after the mode set the SSDK's MHT
+ * GCC clock init (15.262 s) turns SerDes0 off in PHY mode and leaves SerDes1
+ * and the GEPHYs on (stock runtime dump: SRDS0_SYS CBC 0x80000000, SRDS1 and
+ * GEPHY0-3 0x1). Here SerDes0 has no consumer, and the only consumer of
+ * SerDes1, the QCA8084 PCS, enables it at the chip mode set, so
+ * clk_disable_unused() would gate both at about 3.5 s. Hold both on from
+ * here. nss_cc_qca8k_stock_release() drops the SerDes0 hold after the chip
+ * mode set; SerDes1 stays held. The GEPHY system clocks need no hold: each
+ * QCA8084 PHY enables its own at probe, and deferred probes are flushed
+ * (late_initcall) before clk_disable_unused() runs (late_initcall_sync).
+ */
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(nss_cc_qca8k_stock_lock);
+static struct device *nss_cc_qca8k_stock_dev;
+static struct regmap *nss_cc_qca8k_stock_regmap;
+static struct clk *nss_cc_qca8k_srds0_held;
+static bool nss_cc_qca8k_stock_released;
+
+static void nss_cc_qca8k_clk_off(void *clk)
+{
+	clk_disable_unprepare(clk);
+}
+
+/* devm, on unbind: drop the SerDes0 hold if it is still there */
+static void nss_cc_qca8k_stock_forget(void *unused)
+{
+	mutex_lock(&nss_cc_qca8k_stock_lock);
+	if (nss_cc_qca8k_srds0_held)
+		clk_disable_unprepare(nss_cc_qca8k_srds0_held);
+	nss_cc_qca8k_srds0_held = NULL;
+	nss_cc_qca8k_stock_regmap = NULL;
+	nss_cc_qca8k_stock_dev = NULL;
+	mutex_unlock(&nss_cc_qca8k_stock_lock);
+}
+
+static struct clk *nss_cc_qca8k_hold(struct device *dev,
+				     struct clk_branch *branch, const char *name)
+{
+	struct clk *clk;
+	int ret;
+
+	clk = devm_clk_hw_get_clk(dev, &branch->clkr.hw, "stock-order");
+	if (IS_ERR(clk)) {
+		dev_warn(dev, "stock order: %s system clock not held: %ld\n",
+			 name, PTR_ERR(clk));
+		return NULL;
+	}
+
+	ret = clk_prepare_enable(clk);
+	dev_info(dev, "stock order: %s system clock held on: %d\n", name, ret);
+	return ret ? NULL : clk;
+}
+
+static void nss_cc_qca8k_hold_stock(struct device *dev, struct regmap *regmap)
+{
+	struct clk *clk;
+
+	if (!ipq_pcs_stock_order() ||
+	    !device_property_read_bool(dev, "qcom,vendor-preinit"))
+		return;
+
+	clk = nss_cc_qca8k_hold(dev, &nss_cc_srds1_sys_clk, "SerDes1");
+	if (clk && devm_add_action_or_reset(dev, nss_cc_qca8k_clk_off, clk))
+		dev_warn(dev, "stock order: SerDes1 system clock hold dropped\n");
+
+	mutex_lock(&nss_cc_qca8k_stock_lock);
+	/* A built-in QCA8084 PHY driver cannot call into this driver as a
+	 * module, so it could never drop the SerDes0 hold.
+	 */
+	if (IS_MODULE(CONFIG_IPQ_NSSCC_QCA8K) && IS_BUILTIN(CONFIG_QCA808X_PHY))
+		dev_info(dev, "stock order: SerDes0 system clock not held, the built-in QCA8084 PHY driver could not release it\n");
+	else if (!nss_cc_qca8k_stock_released)
+		nss_cc_qca8k_srds0_held = nss_cc_qca8k_hold(dev, &nss_cc_srds0_sys_clk,
+							    "SerDes0");
+	nss_cc_qca8k_stock_dev = dev;
+	nss_cc_qca8k_stock_regmap = regmap;
+	mutex_unlock(&nss_cc_qca8k_stock_lock);
+	if (devm_add_action_or_reset(dev, nss_cc_qca8k_stock_forget, NULL))
+		dev_warn(dev, "stock order: SerDes0 system clock hold dropped\n");
+
+	dev_info(dev, "stock order: GEPHY0-3 system clocks not held, each PHY enables its own at probe\n");
+}
+
+/**
+ * nss_cc_qca8k_stock_release() - stock order: SerDes0 system clock off
+ *
+ * Called by the QCA8084 PHY driver right after its UQXGMII mode set, where
+ * the SSDK's MHT GCC clock init turns the SerDes0 system clock off. Drops the
+ * SerDes0 hold taken at probe; the SerDes1 hold stays. The open SSDK source
+ * asserts the SerDes0 ARES there, but the stock qca-ssdk.ko (1.1.38) calls
+ * ssdk_mht_clk_disable(), which only clears the enable bit, and the stock
+ * runtime dump reads 0x80000000 (enable and ARES clear). So only the clock
+ * goes off, the reset is left alone. Runs once per boot.
+ */
+void nss_cc_qca8k_stock_release(void)
+{
+	unsigned int s0 = 0, s1 = 0, s0_after = 0, s1_after = 0;
+	struct regmap *regmap;
+	bool held;
+
+	mutex_lock(&nss_cc_qca8k_stock_lock);
+	if (nss_cc_qca8k_stock_released)
+		goto out;
+	nss_cc_qca8k_stock_released = true;
+
+	regmap = nss_cc_qca8k_stock_regmap;
+	if (!regmap) {
+		pr_info("qca8k-nsscc: stock order: no clock hold to release after the chip mode set\n");
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	held = !!nss_cc_qca8k_srds0_held;
+	regmap_read(regmap, 0x1a8, &s0);
+	regmap_read(regmap, 0x1ac, &s1);
+	if (held)
+		clk_disable_unprepare(nss_cc_qca8k_srds0_held);
+	nss_cc_qca8k_srds0_held = NULL;
+	regmap_read(regmap, 0x1a8, &s0_after);
+	regmap_read(regmap, 0x1ac, &s1_after);
+	dev_info(nss_cc_qca8k_stock_dev,
+		 "stock order: SerDes0 system clock %s after the chip mode set: SRDS0_SYS_CBCR 0x%08x -> 0x%08x (stock 0x80000000), SRDS1_SYS_CBCR 0x%08x -> 0x%08x (stock 0x1)\n",
+		 held ? "released" : "was not held", s0, s0_after, s1, s1_after);
+out:
+	mutex_unlock(&nss_cc_qca8k_stock_lock);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(nss_cc_qca8k_stock_release);
+
 /* Vendor order (SSDK 3c58ee12): the package is reset and prepared only once
  * the SoC UNIPHY transmits. When the DT names that PCS, wait for it to bind.
  */
@@ -2260,6 +2390,16 @@
 	if (!np)
 		return 0;
 
+	/* Stock order: stock mdio-qca resets the chip at its own probe, long
+	 * before the SSDK touches UNIPHY0. The PCS module parameter is set
+	 * before any initcall, so reading it here has no probe order issue.
+	 */
+	if (ipq_pcs_stock_order()) {
+		of_node_put(np);
+		dev_info_once(dev, "stock order: chip reset at clock controller probe, not after the PCS\n");
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	pcs = of_find_device_by_node(np);
 	of_node_put(np);
 	bound = pcs && device_is_bound(&pcs->dev);
@@ -2288,7 +2428,13 @@
 
 	nss_cc_qca8k_preinit(&mdiodev->dev, regmap);
 
-	return qcom_cc_really_probe(&mdiodev->dev, &nss_cc_qca8k_desc, regmap);
+	ret = qcom_cc_really_probe(&mdiodev->dev, &nss_cc_qca8k_desc, regmap);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	nss_cc_qca8k_hold_stock(&mdiodev->dev, regmap);
+
+	return 0;
 }
 
 static const struct of_device_id nss_cc_qca8k_match_table[] = {
